.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
.TH GIT-CARVE 1 "2026-11-14" "git-carve 1.0.0" "Git Manual"
.SH NAME
git-carve \- carve changes into clean, one-change-per-commit series
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git carve
.RI [ base ..]
.br
.B git carve keys
.br
.B git carve
.RB ( \-h " | " \-\-help " | " \-V " | " \-\-version )
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git carve
stages and unstages single lines, splits commits and moves changes
between the commits of a series. Every commit then does one thing, and
the series stays bisectable.
It is a terminal UI. Everything is done with keys, and
.B ?
lists them.
.PP
git carve runs the
.B git
binary for everything, and your configuration, hooks and filters apply.
Output it parses is protected against configuration that would change
it, such as diff prefixes, colors, external diff drivers, textconv and
quoting. Staging uses
.B git apply --whitespace=nowarn
to keep
.B apply.whitespace=fix
from altering staged content.
.SH THE TERMINAL UI
.B git carve
.RI [ base ..]
opens its panes, cycled with Tab and Shift-Tab:
.BR Series ,
.B Files
(Staged, Unstaged and Untracked sections with status letters, in the
order of
.BR "git status" ),
.B Message
(the commit message being written) and
.BR Diff ,
the diff of the selected file. Diff lines show their old and new line
numbers.
.PP
Every pane has a frame with its title in the top line. The active pane's
frame has heavy lines in colour and names the pane Tab goes to next.
Without UTF-8 all frames are thin and the active one is bold, and with
.B carve.asciiLines
it is drawn with
.B =
and
.BR # .
.PP
The Diff pane shows the diff of the section the cursor is in. A file of
the Unstaged section shows its unstaged changes (the
.B Unstaged
pane below), a file of the Staged section its staged ones (the
.B Staged
pane), and a commit's file the commit's diff. Space moves a line from
one to the other, and the file then shows in the other section. With
.B carve.layout
=
.B two
both diffs are shown, the Unstaged pane above the Staged pane.
.PP
The Message pane holds the commit message while you stage. It is
read-only. It grows with the message up to a third of the screen, at
most 12 lines, and a longer message shows a thumb on the right line of
its frame, where the movement keys scroll it. In it
.B e
opens your editor on the message. An empty one starts from what
.B c
would start the editor with. That is the subject prefix, your sign-off
and during a split the original message.
.B c
then commits with it, without the editor, and empties it. Without a
message
.B c
opens the editor as before. The message is kept in
.B $GIT_DIR/CARVE_MESSAGE
(per worktree), and quitting does not lose it.
When the Series pane shows a commit, the Message pane shows that
commit's message
.RB ( "Message of ~2" ),
and
.B e
there gives it a new message, as
.B r
does.
.B gc
hides the pane or shows it again.
.PP
The Files pane groups each section's files by directory. A dim row
names the directory, and its files below show their names. A path too
long for the pane is cut in the middle, as file browsers do. Its first
directory and its end stay
.RB ( Documentation/\[u2026]/Requirements/ ,
or
.B ~
without UTF-8), and
.B h
shows the rest. Section names are bold, in the colours of
.BR "git status" ,
with unstaged red, staged green and the commit shown cyan.
.PP
Unstaged lists tracked files only. Untracked files come last, folded
to one row with their number, and a tree full of build output does not
bury the changes. Enter (or l, Right) on that row lists them, and h
(Left) or Enter folds them again. During a split the new files of the
commit being split are untracked again. They are the work being split,
and they are listed under Unstaged.
.PP
When an operation is refused or fails, a box in the middle of the
screen says why, wrapped to the terminal, with what to do below the
reason. Output of git that explains a failure, such as a commit hook's,
is in the box too, and the arrow keys scroll it when it is long. Any
other key closes the box and does nothing else. A key typed ahead never
acts on a screen you have not read. Warnings, such as a key
binding that could not be applied, come in the same box. Other
messages, and searches or jumps that find nothing, use the bottom line.
.PP
Below
.B carve.narrowWidth
columns the panes stack. Series and Files take the whole width on top,
with the message and the diff below.
.PP
The Series pane shows your branch, newest first. Your series is
.BR @{upstream}..HEAD .
Without an upstream it is the commits no remote-tracking branch has yet.
.B "git carve HEAD~10.."
gives another base. Absorb and squash use the same series. The pane
starts with the work tree, then
.BR HEAD ,
.BR ~1 ,
.B ~2
and further. A divider marks where the series ends, with the upstream's
name or "pushed", and
.B M
marks a merge. Each commit names the branches at it after its id, as
.B "git log"
does, with local branches green and remote-tracking ones red. The
branch HEAD is on is in the top line. The pane's title says what the
other panes show, the work tree, HEAD or a commit below it
.RB ( "Series: ~2 below HEAD" ),
and the upper diff pane's title names the commit
.RB ( "Commit ~2: a.c" ).
.B J
and
.B K
move from any pane without checking anything out, and
.B *
marks the row shown. A commit's files come into the Files pane as a
.B Commit
section above your own changes, and its diff into the upper pane. Moving
past the last commit loads
.B carve.seriesStep
older ones. With the cursor in it the pane widens to show whole
subjects. The status line always has the whole subject of the commit
shown. A clean work tree shows HEAD and changes in it show the work
tree, until a row is chosen. Opening it is one
.B git log
whatever its size. Two columns in front of each diff row show the cursor
.RB ( > )
and a marked row
.RB ( + )
or a row of the range
.RB ( * ),
both on the cursor row. The pane title counts the marked lines.
In a changed line paired with one on the other side (as partial staging
pairs them), the changed words are shown in reverse video (bold and
underlined without colours).
Whitespace errors on added lines (trailing whitespace, spaces before a
tab in the indent) are highlighted as by
.BR "git diff" ,
following
.BR core.whitespace .
.TP
.BR j " / " k ", arrows"
Move.
.TP
.BR gg " / " G ", Home / End"
To the top / bottom.
.TP
.BR Ctrl-F " / " Ctrl-B ", " PgDn " / " PgUp
A page down / up. In tmux with its default prefix,
.B Ctrl-B
reaches git carve when pressed twice.
.TP
.BR Ctrl-D " / " Ctrl-U
Half a page down / up.
.TP
.BR Ctrl-E " / " Ctrl-Y
Scroll the view a line down / up. The cursor keeps its line while it
is in view.
.TP
.BR zt " / " zz " / " zb
Scroll until the cursor line is at the top / middle / bottom.
.TP
.BR H " / " M " / " L
The cursor to the top / middle / bottom line of the view.
.TP
.BR 0 " / " $
Scroll long lines back to their start / to the end of the cursor line.
.TP
.BR + " (or " = ") / " \-
More / fewer context lines, down to 0. Staging keeps working.
.TP
.B W
Whole functions around the changes
.RB ( "git diff \-W" )
on / off.
.TP
.B w
Ignore whitespace changes on / off. This is a view only. While it is on,
line operations (stage, discard, fixup-to, and edit on a commit's
lines) are refused. The pane
titles show the options that differ from the default.
.TP
.BR h " / " l ", Left / Right"
Show more of the left / right of long lines, as the view moves in
.BR less .
.B h
brings the start of lines into view, and the text moves right.
A mark at the edge of a row says that its text goes on there:
.B \(fo
on the left,
.B \(fc
on the right
.RB ( <
and
.B >
without UTF-8). In the Files pane long paths show their end at first
(the file name), and
.B h
goes towards the start of the selected file and its directory's row. In
the Series pane subjects show their start, and
.B l
goes towards the end of the selected one. The other rows stay as they
are, and moving the cursor puts the row back.
.TP
.BR ] " / " [
Next / previous hunk, or in an unmerged file's view the next / previous
conflict.
.TP
.BR ) " / " (
Next / previous block of changed lines.
.TP
.BR } " / " {
From a diff pane, the next / previous file with changes in that pane,
at its first change. All motions extend a
.B v
range.
.TP
.B Space
Stage (Unstaged pane) or unstage (Staged pane) the current line or
range. On a hunk header it takes the whole hunk, and on a diff that only
allows whole-file operations the file. The cursor then goes on to the
next changed line, and Space after Space goes through a block of
changes.
.TP
.B v
Start or stop a range selection.
.TP
.B m
Mark or unmark the changed line under the cursor. While lines are
marked,
.BR Space ,
.BR t ,
and
.B e
on a commit's lines act on exactly the marked lines. They need not be
next to each other, such as the old and new version of one line in a
block where git lists all removals before the additions.
.B gm
marks a changed line together with the line it is paired with on the
other side (the old and the new version of one line), or unmarks both.
The status bar shows this shortcut when the diff cursor is on a paired line.
.B Esc
clears the marks.
.TP
.B R
In the Files pane, delete the selected file and stage its deletion
.RB ( "git rm" )
after asking.
.B u
undoes it.
.TP
.B gd
Load the diff of the selected file although it is not loaded on its own
(see
.BR "BIG FILES" ).
.TP
.B gi
Add an untracked file to
.B .git/info/exclude
after asking. It is then not listed again.
.TP
.B a
Select the whole hunk.
.TP
.BI : N ", :n" N ", :o" N
Go to new-side line
.IR N ,
or old-side with
.BR o .
These are the same forms as selections.
.TP
.BI ":s/" pattern / replacement / "\fR[\fBgi\fR], :%s/" ...
Replace in the work tree, as vim does. It acts on the marked lines, the
range, or the line or hunk under the cursor of the Unstaged pane or of
the commit shown (its line in the work tree). With
.B %
it acts on the whole file. The pattern is a POSIX extended regular
expression. In the replacement
.B &
is the match and
.BR \e1 .. \e9
are its groups. The flags are
.B g
for every match on a line and
.B i
to ignore case.
.B u
undoes it. For a small edit without an editor, use for example
.B :s/100/500/
on a line of HEAD shown in the Commit section, then stage and amend.
.TP
.BR / ", " n ", " N
Search the diffs of all changed files, then go to the next and previous
hit. The search goes on into the next or previous file and wraps
around. The Unstaged pane searches unstaged changes and the lines of
conflicts, and the Staged pane staged ones. Untracked files are not
searched. A pattern without capitals ignores case. A search that needs
git shows that while it runs, and any key stops it. With more than
3,000 changed files it starts in the file shown and reads the other
files' diffs only when it leaves it.
.TP
.BR / " (Files pane), " Esc
Filter the Files list to the files whose path matches, in every
section. A rename matches by either path, and a pattern without capitals
ignores case. The list changes as the pattern is typed. Untracked files
that match are listed even when their section is folded. Enter keeps
the filter and shows it in the pane's title, and an empty pattern clears
it. Esc in the prompt puts back the filter before, and Esc in the Files
pane clears it. No git runs for it.
.TP
.B g/
Every hit as
.IR path : n12: text ,
to jump to.
.TP
.B e
Edit what the cursor is on. In the Files, Unstaged and Staged panes
that is the file, opened in the editor at the current line. On the
lines of a commit shown from the Series pane it is those lines, as the
commit has them. In the Series pane it is the commit's message, as
.B r
gives, and on the work tree row the message of the next commit, as in
the Message pane.
.TP
.BR O ", " T
Take our / their side of a conflict. The Unstaged pane of a conflicted
file lists its conflict regions with the side labels git wrote. During
a rebase "ours" is the rewritten history and "theirs" the commit being
replayed. The keys resolve the region under the cursor, or in the Files
pane the whole file after asking. The conflicts keep
the numbers they were first shown with ("conflict 2 of 2" once the
first is resolved). A file without conflicts left is staged.
.TP
.B d
Discard the selected unstaged lines from the work tree after asking. In
the Files pane it discards all unstaged changes of the file, and deletes
an untracked file.
.B u
brings them back.
.TP
.B Enter
In the Files pane, stage or unstage the whole file. The cursor stays in
its section, on the next file. On a section's row
.RB ( Staged ", " Unstaged )
Enter (or
.BR Space )
stages or unstages all its files at once. With a filter it takes the
ones the filter shows, and files with conflicts are left out. On the
Untracked row Space stages all untracked files and Enter folds the row. With
.B v
in the Files pane, Enter takes a range of files. Above 100 files it
asks first, and
.B u
takes it all back in one step.
.B gg
goes to the first file,
.B k
from there to the section's row.
.TP
.BR c ", " A
Commit, amend HEAD. The editor starts with the subsystem prefix the
history of the staged files uses most, such as
.BR "net: phy: " .
.B carve.suggestPrefix
turns that off. During a split it starts from a template (see
SPLITTING).
.TP
.B Ctrl-S
Sign-off on or off. Commits and amends get the committer's
.B Signed-off-by
trailer unless it is already there. It starts as
.B carve.signoff
says, and the header shows
.B sign-off
while it is on.
.TP
.B s
Split HEAD, or the older commit shown in the Commit section. The
commits above it are replayed when it is finished.
.B ZZ
finishes the split.
.TP
.BR J ", " K
The next (older, below) commit of the Series pane, and the previous
(newer, above) one, up to the work tree. The tip is on top, and
commits are named from it
.RB ( HEAD
(or
.BR tip ),
.BR ~1 ,
.BR ~2 ).
.TP
.B t
Fixup-to ("to"). Move the selected lines (Unstaged pane), everything
staged (Staged pane) or the selected file into an older commit chosen
from a list, at once. The commits above are replayed. In the list,
.B b
goes to the commit that last touched the lines (see
.BR f ).
.TP
.B o
Zoom the active pane (Files, Series or a diff) to the whole screen,
and back.
.B Tab
goes to the next pane, still zoomed. The zoom stays on from commit to
commit
.RB ( J " / " K ).
.TP
.B Ctrl-W
Resize the panes.
.BR h " and " l
(or the arrow keys) make the lists narrower or wider,
.BR j " and " k
move the divider of the active pane down or up. That is the one between
Series and Files, or between the two diffs, and on a narrow terminal
also the one between the lists and the diffs.
.B Tab
picks another pane.
.B =
puts the default layout back, and Enter or Esc ends resizing. The sizes
last until git carve quits and stay within the screen when it is
resized.
.TP
.B gS
Hide the Series pane, or show it again
.RB ( J " and " K
still move through the commits).
.TP
.B gD
Show one file at a time in the diff pane, or the diffs of all files of
a section one after another again, as at the start
.RB ( carve.allFiles ).
Each file starts with a bar with its status,
path and how many lines it adds and removes. Moving past the end of a
file goes on into the next one and the Files cursor follows, and the
keys act on the file the cursor is in.
.BR } " and " {
jump to the next and previous file. The files after it come from the
diff cache, without running git. While the cache is being read they
show as loading, and a file it does not hold loads when the cursor gets
to it. After the last change of a file is
staged, the next file follows. A range stays within one file, and
.B gg
and
.B G
go to the top and the bottom of the file.
.TP
.B D
Show the commit shown in the Commit section, else HEAD, with the id,
the subject, trailers, the stat and the diff coloured as
.B git show
colours them. Text windows like this one scroll with the movement keys
and say which lines they show. The list of search hits and absorb's
plan are coloured too.
.B o
puts such a window on the whole screen, and the next ones open that way
until
.B o
makes them smaller again.
.TP
.B ZZ
Finish what is in progress. That is a split, or a move or fixup-to
whose conflict was resolved. A split finishes once nothing is left to
stage and the new commits add up to the original commit exactly, less
the lines discarded with
.B d
during the split. The commits above it are then replayed and must keep
their contents.
.TP
.B ga
Absorb. Show where each unstaged change block would go, the commit of
the series that last touched its lines, or why it stays. Enter does it.
With neither an upstream nor a remote-tracking branch the series is the
newest 200 commits.
.TP
.B X
Give up what is in progress, such as a split, a move or fixup-to.
Everything is as it was.
.TP
.B u
Undo the last change to the index or HEAD (see
.BR UNDO ).
It asks before forcing when things changed since.
.TP
.B r
A new message in the editor for the commit shown in the Commit section,
or for HEAD when none is shown. The commits above it are replayed.
.TP
.B f
Make a
.B fixup!
commit for a commit chosen from a list. The list starts on the commit
shown in the Series pane. The fixup holds what is staged. With nothing
staged it takes the selected lines (Unstaged pane) or the selected file
(Files pane), staged once the commit is chosen. Nothing is rewritten.
The fixups are squashed into their commits later
.RB ( "git rebase \-\-autosquash" ).
In the list,
.B b
(blame) goes to the commit that last touched the lines of the change
and says how many of them come from it. It runs
.B "git blame \-M"
of HEAD once, on the removed lines, the lines an added line replaces, or
the lines around an insertion. It blames the selected lines of the file
shown, else its whole change, or with something staged the file's
staged change.
.TP
.B F
Make an
.B amend!
commit for a commit chosen from a list. The editor starts from that
commit's message, and the squash replaces the message with the edited
one. It holds what is staged, if anything. Unstaged lines are not taken
along.
.TP
.B S
Squash the
.BR fixup! ,
.B amend!
and
.B squash!
commits of the series into their commits, as
.B git rebase \-\-autosquash
does, after saying how many go into how many commits. Each goes right
after the commit its subject names. A fixup keeps that commit's
message, an amend! replaces it (on every git version) and a squash!
adds its own to it. A fixup whose commit is not in the series stays
where it is. The series ends with the same content as before, and your
staged and unstaged changes stay as they are. A conflict stops as
fixup-to's does, and
.B u
undoes a finished squash.
.TP
.BR q ", " ?
Quit, and help. The help lists the keys of the active pane in effect,
one line each, grouped by kind of work. The actions come first
(staging, selecting, committing, splitting, the Series pane) and moving
around last.
.B ?
again lists all keys with their long descriptions, and
.B /
searches all keys by name, key and description. Enter runs the action
of the row with the cursor, as its key would.
.TP
.BR Ctrl-L ", " F5
Repaint the screen and read the status, the diffs and the
configuration again.
.B F5
only reads them again.
.TP
.B Ctrl-Z
Suspend to the shell.
.B fg
returns to a redrawn screen, also after a resize in between. Below
40x10 the TUI only shows how much room it needs.
.PP
New untracked files can be staged line by line. The first staged line
marks the file intent-to-add. Binary files, pure mode changes and
deletions allow whole-file operations only.
.PP
A rename is one entry,
.BR "R old -> new" ,
also when the file was only moved in the work tree. Such a move is
found in a temporary copy of the index, and the real one is not
touched. Its diff is the content change against the old file. Staging
the entry stages only the move, with the old content at the new path,
and the edits stay unstaged as ordinary line changes. Staging lines of
it stages the move and the lines. Unstaging a staged rename takes both
halves, and discarding one deletes the new file and brings the old one
back. A new file that
is an exact copy of a file in HEAD is noted as such.
.PP
These are the default keys. Every one can be changed with
.BR carve.keys. \fIaction\fR
(see CONFIGURATION).
.B git carve keys
and
.B ?
show the keys in effect.
.SH BIG FILES
The TUI never waits for a diff it cannot use. Files matching
.B carve.skipDiff
(tag files, unless configured otherwise) and whole-file diffs over
.B carve.maxFileSize
are not loaded. The pane says why, and whole-file operations work
.RB ( Space ,
.BR Enter ).
.B gd
loads the diff anyway, and
.B gi
hides an untracked file for good. A diff over
.B carve.diffLimit
lines stops git as soon as it gets there. A load that takes longer than
a moment shows that in the status line, and any key stops it. The file
then stays unloaded until another file is selected or
.B gd
is pressed. Search leaves skipped files out and stops the same way.
.SH SPLITTING
The commit being split is kept in
.BR refs/worktree/carve/orig
per worktree, and a split in one worktree is nothing to the others.
The first commit made during the split starts with the original
message. Later ones start empty, with the original message as a
commented reference. If the original message has a Gerrit
.B Change-Id:
trailer it is kept in exactly one commit (see
.BR carve.keepChangeId ).
The others get new ids from your commit-msg hook.
Every commit of the split keeps the original's
.B Signed-off-by:
lines. The later ones have them in their templates, and a message given
with
.B \-m
gets them too.
.BR Reviewed-by: ,
.B Acked-by:
and
.B Tested-by:
were given for the patch before the split, and no part keeps them.
Starting the split prints a one-line note naming the ones dropped.
.SS Changing any commit of the series
The commit shown in the Commit section (the Series pane,
.BR J / K )
changes from its diff, without stopping there first.
.B Space
(or
.B Enter
on its file in the Files pane) takes the selected lines out of it into
the work tree, unstaged.
.B d
drops them, from the work tree too, after asking.
.B t
moves them into another commit, earlier or later, chosen from the
series, or into a new commit in one of the gaps between them. The
editor then asks for the new commit's message. It starts from the
subsystem prefix, and comment lines below a scissors line name the
lines moved and the commit they came from. The commit runs with
.BR \-\-cleanup=scissors ,
and a line of the message that starts with the comment character stays.
.B e
edits the lines as the commit has them,
.B r
gives the commit a new message and
.B s
splits it. Each is one
.B git rebase \-i
with a break after every commit it touches, and HEAD alone needs none.
The same checks as for fixup-to apply. The tip's tree must be what the
change makes of it. For
.B Space
and
.B d
that is the tree without the lines. Otherwise everything is rolled
back. Your own changes, staged and not, are saved first and put back as
they were. A conflict, where a later commit
touches the same lines, asks whether to resolve it now or give the
change up. After resolving,
.B ZZ
goes on and
.B X
gives up.
.SS In a rebase of yours
Where a rebase you started stops cleanly
.RB ( edit ,
.BR break ),
HEAD is split
.RB ( s )
and lines are carved out of it
.RB ( t
on its lines, then a gap next to it)
as anywhere else. The TUI shows the commit in the Files pane as for a
commit of the Series pane, with the rebase's step in the status line
.RB ( "REBASE 3/7" ).
The Series pane shows what the rebase replays
.RB ( onto..HEAD ),
and the move picker names its gaps by the commit above them
.RB ( "a new commit before 1a2b3c4" ).
Lines move into a commit below HEAD, or a new commit between two, at
once. git cannot rebase inside your rebase. The move runs in a worktree
of its own under
.BR /carve ,
removed afterwards, and HEAD then points at the result. The result must
have the tree your rebase stopped at. Nothing is left to do later.
.B git rebase \-\-abort
drops the move with everything else the rebase did. A move that does
not apply, because a commit between changes the lines around it, says
why and changes nothing. Staged changes at the stop still go into the
commit it stopped at on
.BR "git rebase \-\-continue" .
When git carve rewrites HEAD there with a move, a carve or lines taken
out,
.B rebase\-merge/amend
follows it. That file only names the commit git amends staged changes
into. The new HEAD is that commit with new parents, or after a carve
after it the commit you now stand on. Pointing the file there is what
git would do had the commit been edited by hand.
On a big tree the worktree costs a checkout.
The rebase is left to you. Finishing a split does not continue it, and
.B git rebase \-\-continue
goes on afterwards with the same tip tree. What needs a rebase of its
own, such as splitting an older commit, fixup-to, defer or absorb, is
refused until yours is done. In a conflict the commit is not made yet.
Stage parts of it, commit them, and
.B git rebase \-\-continue
commits the rest with the original message.
.SH UNDO
Every action that changes the index or HEAD records an undo entry. That
covers staging and unstaging, commit, amend, split, moving lines,
fixups, squash, discarding lines and taking a side of a conflict.
.B u
reverts the newest entry, and HEAD and the index go back to what they
were before it. Undoing a commit is
.BR "git reset \-\-soft" ,
and its changes are staged again. The work tree is only touched to undo
a discard or a side taken in a conflict. The files are saved in the
entry first, and undo asks before it overwrites a file that changed
since.
Undoing
.B O
or
.B T
puts the conflict back as it was, with the markers in the file and the
path unmerged in the index. An intent-to-add mark is not restored, and
the file is untracked again with its content.
.PP
The entries of stage and unstage keys typed in quick succession are
written together when the keys pause for a second, before any other
action is recorded, before an undo and on quitting. Each key is still
one entry. A line staged or unstaged is kept as the patch it applied,
and undone by applying it the other way round. That is refused if the
lines changed since.
.PP
An operation that takes several steps, such as a split or a move that
stopped on a conflict, becomes a single entry once it finishes. While
it is in progress the steps inside it can be undone one by one, and
.B X
undoes the operation itself.
.PP
When HEAD or the index changed since the entry was recorded, for
example by plain git commands, undo asks before it restores the
recorded state anyway. Entries are refs under
.B refs/worktree/carve/undo/
(per worktree). They point at commits that keep the earlier index and
HEAD reachable, and
.B git gc
leaves them alone. Only the newest
.B carve.undoLimit
entries are kept.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.TP
.B carve.keepChangeId
Which commit of a split keeps the original Change-Id:
.B first
(default),
.B last
or
.BR none .
.TP
.B carve.tabWidth
Tab width in the TUI diff panes (default 8).
.TP
.B carve.asciiLines
Draw the frames of the panes with ASCII characters instead of
line-drawing ones.
This is for terminals or locales that show them as garbage, often over
ssh.
Without it curses already falls back to ASCII where the terminal has no
line drawing.
.TP
.B carve.escDelay
How many milliseconds
.B Esc
waits for the rest of a key such as an arrow. An arrow arrives as Esc
and more characters. The default is 100 and the most is 1000. Raise it
when arrows act
as Esc over a slow link. The
.B ESCDELAY
environment variable, as ncurses reads it, wins.
.TP
.B carve.colors
.B 8
(the default) uses the 8 basic colours, and your terminal's theme
decides their shades.
.B 256
uses a fixed palette for dark terminal backgrounds where the
terminal has 256 colours. That is a
.B TERM
such as xterm-256color, or tmux-256color in tmux. Elsewhere the basic
colours are used.
.B NO_COLOR
turns colours off either way.
.TP
.B carve.untrackedFiles
.B async
(the default) shows tracked changes at once and adds untracked files
when a background scan finishes.
.B no
does not list untracked files at all, the fastest choice on huge trees.
.B core.fsmonitor
and
.B core.untrackedCache
are always honoured. With either set the scan is
.BR "git status" ,
and git uses them. Without them it is
.BR "git ls-files \-o" .
That lists the same files without refreshing the index. A refresh takes
seconds after a reset of a big tree. The Files pane's title shows the
cursor's place among the files
.RB ( "Files: 1203/14914" ).
.TP
.B carve.diffLimit
The most diff lines a TUI pane shows. The default is 100000, and 0 means
no limit. git is stopped as soon as a diff goes over it. The pane shows
a note, and
.B Space
then stages or unstages the whole file.
.TP
.B carve.maxFileSize
A diff that is a whole file, such as a new, untracked or deleted file,
is not loaded in the TUI when it is larger than this. The default is
5m. The suffixes k, m and g work, and 0 means no limit. Only the
file's size is looked at, never its content.
.TP
.B carve.skipDiff
A pattern of files whose diff the TUI does not load and search leaves
out. It can be given several times. As in
.BR .gitignore ,
a pattern without a slash matches the file name in any directory, and
one with a slash the path from the top. The globs are
.BR * ,
.B ?
and
.BR [...] ,
without
.BR ** .
Unset, it is the tag files editors and code browsers generate,
.BR tags ,
.BR TAGS ,
.BR cscope.out ,
.BR cscope.*.out ,
.BR GTAGS ,
.BR GRTAGS ,
.BR GPATH .
An empty value clears the list so far.
.TP
.B carve.signoff
Start with sign-off on, as
.B Ctrl-S
turns it on. The default is false.
.TP
.B carve.suggestPrefix
Start commit messages with the subsystem prefix the history of the
staged files uses most (default true).
.TP
.B carve.seriesStep
How many older commits the Series pane loads each time the cursor
passes the last one (default 5).
.TP
.B carve.undoLimit
How many undo entries to keep. The default is 50, and 0 turns
recording off.
.TP
.B carve.seriesPane
Whether the TUI starts with the Series pane shown. The default is true,
and
.B gS
toggles it.
.TP
.B carve.narrowWidth
Below this many columns the TUI stacks its panes, with the lists
across the whole width on top and the diffs below. The default is 100.
.TP
.B carve.layout
.B one
(the default) shows one Diff pane with the diff of the cursor's
section.
.B two
shows both the Unstaged and the Staged diff.
.TP
.B carve.allFiles
Whether the diff pane shows the diffs of all files one after another at
the start. The default is true, and
.B gD
toggles it.
.TP
.B carve.messagePane
Whether the Message pane is shown at the start. The default is true,
and
.B gc
toggles it.
.TP
.B carve.absorbCopies
Whether absorb also follows lines copied or moved from other files
.RB ( "git blame \-C" ).
The default is false. absorb then follows only lines moved within a
file, several times faster on a big tree.
.TP
.BI carve.keys. action
Space separated keys for a TUI action, replacing its defaults. For
example
.B git config carve.keys.stage "y Space"
or in a config file
.RS
.nf
[carve "keys"]
	stage = y Space
	quit = Ctrl-Q
	squash = none
.fi
.RE
.IP
Action names are listed by
.BR "git carve keys" .
Keys are single characters or
.BR Space   Tab   Shift-Tab   Esc   Enter   Backspace ,
.BR Up   Down   Left   Right   PgUp   PgDn   Home   End ,
.BR Ins   Del   Ctrl-A " to " Ctrl-Z ", " F1 " to " F12 ,
and sequences of two characters such as
.B gg
The first key of a sequence waits for the second, and the status line
lists what can follow it.
.B Esc
cancels. A plain key bound to the first key of a sequence ends the
default sequences that start with it, and git carve reports that.
.B none
leaves the action without a key. A key already taken by another
configured action, or an unknown name, is reported when the TUI starts
and the action keeps its defaults. The arrow keys always move.
Every text of the TUI that names a key names the key in effect. That
covers the status line, messages, notes in the panes and the help. An
action without a key shows as
.IR <action> .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set and not empty, the TUI uses no colours. As on a monochrome
terminal, markers
.RB ( > ,
.BR * ,
.BR + )
and reverse or bold text keep every state visible.
.TP
.B GIT_CARVE_TRACE
A file that gets one line for every git process git carve runs, with
how long it took and its arguments.
.B bg
marks the background scan of untracked files. It shows what an
operation costs.
.TP
.B GIT_CARVE_IDLE
A file that gets
.B idle
whenever the TUI has nothing left to do but wait for a key, and
.B busy
when a key comes. The tests use it to know when the work of their keys
is done.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B $GIT_DIR/carve/
State of the split, fixup-to or defer in progress.
.TP
.BR refs/worktree/carve/orig ", " refs/worktree/carve/worktree
The commit being split, and the saved work tree during a fixup-to.
.SH EXAMPLES
Stage one line out of a hunk and commit it. Select the line in the
Unstaged pane, press
.BR Space ,
then
.BR c .
.PP
Split the third commit from the top. Press
.B J J J
in any pane, then
.BR s .
Stage and commit the pieces, then press
.BR ZZ .
.PP
Fix an older commit. Select the lines and press
.BR f ,
then
.B b
to find the commit that last touched them, and Enter. Later
.B S
squashes all such fixups.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-add (1),
.BR git-rebase (1),
.BR git-apply (1)
